---
// Halftone screen plus a riso grain wash, both fixed behind the page.
// Two static CSS layers — nothing here scripts, animates or paints per frame.
---

<div id="ambient-field" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<div id="ambient-grain" aria-hidden="true"></div>

<style>
  /* z-index: -1 keeps both layers behind content without needing a stacking
     context on every section. The page colour is carried by :root so it paints
     below them — see the [data-has-hero] body rule in custom.css. */
  #ambient-field,
  #ambient-grain {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: -1;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* This was a canvas redrawing a document-space dot screen ~5x a second. What
     it actually put on screen is two things: a fine halftone, and a slow swell
     in how present that halftone is from region to region — dense patches,
     thinned bands, near-bare voids. Both are static properties of a picture, so
     the paint loop was buying nothing the page keeps.

     The dots are one gradient tile. The swell is two shallow ramps crossing at
     different angles and periods (520px / 760px); intersecting rather than
     stacking them means a point is dense only where both are high, so the beat
     between their periods reads as irregular clumping instead of plaid. Periods
     under ~500px put more than one cycle on screen at once and the field goes
     evenly busy — the canvas's character is that whole regions go bare, which
     needs a trough wide enough to hold one. Neither ramp reaches zero, so a
     whisper of tooth survives everywhere, as it did under the 0.3 presence
     floor the canvas clamped to.

     --field-rgb and --field-a are unchanged; the script read them off the
     canvas, the mask and opacity below read the same two values.

     Not carried over: dot pitch coarsened 9px to 14px down the *document*, and
     the swell crept a few px/sec. A fixed layer is viewport-space and can hold
     neither. Over one screenful the pitch drift is ~2px and the creep was below
     the threshold the dots themselves read at. */
  #ambient-field {
    --field-swell:
      repeating-linear-gradient(112deg, rgb(0 0 0 / 1) 0px, rgb(0 0 0 / 0.06) 260px, rgb(0 0 0 / 1) 520px),
      repeating-linear-gradient(26deg, rgb(0 0 0 / 1) 0px, rgb(0 0 0 / 0.16) 380px, rgb(0 0 0 / 1) 760px);
    opacity: var(--field-a);
    background-image: radial-gradient(circle, rgba(var(--field-rgb), 0.13) 1.15px, transparent 1.25px);
    background-size: 10px 10px;
    -webkit-mask-image: var(--field-swell);
    mask-image: var(--field-swell);
    -webkit-mask-composite: source-in;
    mask-composite: intersect;
  }

  /* The design ships --riso-amt 0.5-0.7 and --riso-tint-amt 0.12; the export
     wires both grain layers to the former, which composites to a saturated teal
     rather than a texture. 0.12 is the tint-strength value and reads as paper. */
  #ambient-grain {
    opacity: 0.12;
    background-color: var(--mantis-teal);
    -webkit-mask-image: url('/images/riso-noise.png');
    mask-image: url('/images/riso-noise.png');
    -webkit-mask-size: 200px 200px;
    mask-size: 200px 200px;
    -webkit-mask-repeat: repeat;
    mask-repeat: repeat;
  }
</style>
